# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
CHAPTER = {
    "num": 4,
    "title": "The Visual and Auditory Codes: Kinesics and Vocalics",
    "authors": "Burgoon, Manusov & Guerrero",
    "domain": "Nonverbal Communication | Kinesics and Vocalics",
    "scrub": (
        "Scrub the Visual Code: The Scope and Power of Kinesics",
        "Your mission is to scrub through the most powerful of the nonverbal codes: define kinesics, the visible code of body movement that excludes physical contact, and weigh the sheer wealth of its inventory, from Pei's 700,000 physical signs and the face's 20,000 expressions to Krout's 7,777 classroom gestures and Hewes's 1,000 postures. Then test the receiving side: vision's 80 per cent share of sensory perception, movements caught in 1/50 of a second, brain-registered flashes of 125 microseconds, Emerson's ocular dialect, the equifinality of approval signals, and why people rely on kinesic cues more than any other code.",
        "A time-scrubber playhead glides over a medical facility where a consultant scans a body; counters tick up an estimated 700,000 physical signs and 20,000 facial expressions while an eye resolves a 1/50-second flash; Emerson's quotation hangs over a nod, a smile, two thumbs up and fists shaken overhead, all signaling approval.",
        [
            "Kinesics is what we commonly (but problematically) know as 'body language,' and it includes all the ways we use the body to express and interpret messages, making it the most elemental wordless way we communicate with one another.",
            "The term kinesics derives from the Greek word for 'movement' and refers to all forms of body movement, excluding physical contact with another's body, including facial, eye, head, trunk, limb, hand, and foot movements as well as postures and gait (how people walk).",
            "Billions of years before written language was developed, humans were using kinesics and vocalics to communicate (Givens, 2020), and they occupy a central place in the assemblage that is nonverbal communication.",
            "Ralph Waldo Emerson opined that '[t]he eyes of men converse as much as their tongues, with the advantage that the ocular dialect needs no dictionary but is understood the world over.'",
            "The face and eyes are commanding because they are always visible and always 'on'; even in repose or in solitude, the body still presents useful information and can reveal internal mood states or emotions (Cohn & Ekman, 2008).",
            "An estimated 700,000 different physical signs can be produced by humans (Pei, 1965), and physiologists have estimated that the 30 or so muscles in the face are capable of displaying 20,000 different expressions (Birdwhistell, 1970).",
            "Krout (1971) observed 7,777 distinct gestures in classroom behavior and 5,000 hand gestures in clinical situations, while Hewes (1957) reported that 1,000 steady human postures are possible.",
            "Vision accounts for around 80% of our sensory perception; the remaining 20% comes from hearing, touching, tasting, and smelling.",
            "Observers can distinguish movements as short as 1/50 of a second, and even fleeting facial expressions and body movements of 125 microseconds that seem to escape conscious observation may still be registered by the brain (McLeod & Rosenthal, 1983).",
            "Because of equifinality, you might approve of something a friend has done with a very brief, almost imperceptible head nod, a vigorous nod, a smile, two thumbs up, or shaking both arms overhead with hands clenched in fists, each transmitting a slight variation on that theme of approval.",
            "People rely more on kinesic cues than any other code to derive meaning from nonverbal messages (Burgoon & Hoobler, 2002), as a result of the innate tendency for those that are sighted to orient to others visually and the overtly noticeable, attention-getting characteristics of kinesics.",
        ],
    ),
    "dialogue": (
        "Dialogue in Silence: The Zen Parable and the Origins of Kinesics",
        "Your mission is to stage the silent debate between the one-eyed younger monk and the wandering traveler in the temple of northern Japan, then replay both wildly different interpretations of the same raised fingers and clenched fist. Then trace the origins of the kinesic code: Darwin's evolution-wired expressions, the innate tongue display of Michael Jordan, the newborn's Moro reflex and 72-hour-old imitation, intermodal matching, the first-year pointing gesture, gaze fixated on the caregiver's eyes at birth, and the three routes, innate, common-experiential, and culture-specific, by which kinesic expressions are acquired.",
        "A shrine room where two monks sit in silence while one finger, two fingers, three fingers and a clenched fist float between them; each finger is captioned twice, once Buddha's enlightenment and once the younger monk's insult; beneath them, an infant reaches for a caregiver and Michael Jordan's tongue shows at a basketball game.",
        [
            "In a temple in the northern part of Japan two brother monks were dwelling together, and the elder, who was learned, told the younger, who was stupid and had but one eye, to take his place and 'request the dialogue in silence' when a wandering monk challenged them to a debate.",
            "In the traveler's account of the silent debate, his one finger represented Buddha, the younger monk's two fingers signified Buddha and his teachings, three fingers meant Buddha, his teachings and his followers living the harmonious life, and the clenched fist shaken in his face indicated that all three come from one realization, so he concluded the younger monk had defeated him.",
            "In the younger monk's account, the traveler's one finger insultingly insinuated that he had only one eye, his own two fingers congratulated the stranger on having two eyes, the three fingers suggested that between them they only had three eyes, and he got mad and started to punch the traveler before he ran out.",
            "Charles Darwin, in The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals (1872), noted similarities between the emotional expressions of humans and those of primates and other species, and believed that facial expressions were programmed into the brain in the course of human development due to their importance to survival.",
            "Tongue showing is a kinesic behavior with innate origins: when people are intensely involved in activities requiring concentration, are under stress, feel a loss of control, or are being reprimanded, they often display their tongue, as Michael Jordan was known for doing while playing basketball.",
            "As early as 72 hours old, infants imitate adults' tongue protrusions and head movements, and infants imitate facial gestures as early as two months, an imitation reflex that is innate for most infants, requires little cognitive function, and helps newborns maintain social interaction before their control of gaze, head, and mouth movements develops.",
            "Newborns display many kinesic reflexes in the first few months of life, including a ritualized Moro reflex in which they reach for caregivers who, in turn, respond by lifting them up (Rousseau et al., 2017), and within the first three months infants smile, stare at faces, make eye contact, and display distress, delight, and excitement.",
            "The pointing gesture usually emerges in a child's first year and provides a bridge between nonverbal and verbal expression as children acquire more language skills, with children often looking at a caregiver before pointing, as if first checking that the caregiver is paying attention.",
            "Within a few days of birth, infants appear to fixate on the caregiver's eyes, and simply seeing the eyes of a caregiver is sufficient to produce a smiling reaction, with the distance at which newborns focus best being the distance from the infant to the mother's eyes when breast-feeding (Argyle & Cook, 1976).",
            "Eye contact is necessary for proper maturation of infants, and the lack of mutual gaze impairs infant interaction and perceptual and social development (Andersen, 1985).",
            "Kinesic expressions are acquired in three ways: from innate neurological processes passed on genetically and developed through evolution, from experiences common to all humans as they interact with the environment (such as eating with the hands and witnessing death), and through culture-specific tasks and social interactions that vary across cultures.",
            "Mothers are generally children's 'expert' tutors in emotional expressions, and the affective environment created by mothers, their role as models of expressive behavior, and their responsiveness, especially to children's expressions of distress, are particularly important influences on the socialization of emotional expressions (Denham & Grout, 1993).",
        ],
    ),
    "audit": (
        "Audit the Savvy Traveler's Emblems: Cataloguing Gestures With Precise Meanings",
        "Your mission is to audit the emblem, the gesture with a clear symbolic meaning that stands alone as a speech act: check it against the six criteria, tick off what emblems do from the wave hello to the throat-slashing death sign, and count the ledger from Iran's 200 Persian emblems to Israel's 50 and America's fewer than 100. Then take the audit to Rome, where a taxi driver freezes in astonishment, Berlusconi serves Obama a va-va-voom, Poggi counts 250 conversational gestures, and Sraffa's chin-brush rewrites Wittgenstein, and close the books on the Chicago grain traders' buy-and-sell palms and Kita's four cultural dimensions of iconic gestures.",
        "An audit ledger on a world map: six checkboxes for the emblem's functions stamp past, three exhibit cards count 200 Persian, 50 Israeli and fewer than 100 US emblems, a Roman cab freezes a driver with arms flat against his sides, Berlusconi pinches his fingers at the G20, and a trading floor shows a palm inward for buy and a palm outward for sell.",
        [
            "Emblems are gestures that have a clear symbolic meaning and can stand alone as a speech act, meeting criteria that include having a direct verbal translation, having a precise meaning known by most or all members of a social group, being most often used intentionally to transmit a message, and having clear meaning even when displayed out of context.",
            "Emblems do such things as express greetings (a wave hello), requests (the 'come here' beckoning gesture), insults (the cuckold horns), threats (the pistol shot gesture with forefinger and thumb), physical states (stomach rubbing to signal hunger or satiation), death (slashing the throat), and promises ('cross my heart' or crossed fingers to nullify a promise).",
            "Emblems serve the same functions in all cultures, but the number, forms, and meanings of particular emblems differ from culture to culture, as there are 200 Persian emblems in use in current-day Iran but only about 50 in Israel.",
            "Ekman reported that emblems perform six functions in all cultures: insulting others, giving interpersonal directions like 'come here' or 'be quiet', greeting others, signaling departure, replying positively or negatively to requests, and commenting on physical or emotional states.",
            "All cultures seem to use emblems in similar places in conversations: at the beginning or end of a turn, in filled pauses, and preceding or accompanying the words they repeat, though most emblems have unique meanings in each culture.",
            "Some cultures, like middle-class US culture, have less than 100 emblems, whereas others, like Israeli student culture, have over 250, so the savvy traveler should learn in advance what a culture's emblems mean and may consult Desmond Morris's Bodytalk (2015) for the country-specific meanings of various gestures.",
            "Isabella Poggi, a professor of psychology at Roma Tre University and an expert on gestures, has identified around 250 gestures that Italians use in everyday conversation, and the only thing differentiating them from sign language is that they are used individually and lack a full syntax.",
            "When former Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi greeted then-President Obama and his wife, Michelle, at a meeting of the Group of 20 leaders in September 2009, he extended both hands, palms facing toward himself, and then pinched his fingers as he looked Mrs. Obama up and down, a gesture that might be interpreted as 'va-va-voom.'",
            "The philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein overhauled, or at least refined, his theory that language was used to establish truth and inform after the Italian economist Piero Sraffa responded to his theory with a single gesture: fingers brushing the chin, indicating 'I don't give a damn,' the classic antiauthority brushoff.",
            "Davidson (1950) documented emblems used by grain traders on the floor of the Chicago Board of Trade, where the palm of hand held up and inward meant 'buy,' the palm held outward meant 'sell,' fingers held vertically indicated quantities traded (5,000 bushels per finger), and fingers held horizontally indicated price.",
            "Kita (2009) identified four dimensions along which iconic gestures vary across cultures, including culture-specific conventions for associations between form and meaning, such as the cut-off gesture that is more common in Asian than Western cultures and showing modesty by covering one's mouth when laughing.",
            "Emblems are more likely to be displayed when communicating across a noisy room that prevents verbal communication, and many groups, like businesses and professions, develop their own emblems that carry meaning only among members of those groups, such as those used on the floor of the Chicago Board of Trade.",
        ],
    ),
    "matrix": (
        "Matrix Match: Structure Meets Function, From Birdwhistell's Kinemes to the Duchenne Smile",
        "Your mission is to build a two-sided matrix for the kinesic code: drop the structural cards into place, Birdwhistell's 50 to 60 culturally universal kinemes across eight body regions, the kines and allokines like the 23 eyelid positions that reduce to four meaningful ones, the kinemorphs that build sentences, and parakinesic markers, stress, and juncture that work like syntax, then file the Duchenne smile against the posed social smile with its zygomatic major and orbicularis oculi. Then fill the functional cells with Ekman and Friesen's five gesture types, the eight illustrator categories, the three adaptor targets, the regulators' turn-taking gaze norms, and a computer-vision blob that finds the hands in Quadrant 2.",
        "A wall matrix with STRUCTURE on one side and FUNCTION on the other; cards fly into place, a notated face divides into Birdwhistell's eight regions, two smiles face off with lip corners versus eye corners highlighted, five gesture-type cards spread out, a pencil-tapping object adaptor, and a video frame where an ellipse labeled Quadrant 2 tracks a gesturing hand.",
        [
            "In Birdwhistell's system, the smallest meaningful unit of behavior is the kineme, and Birdwhistell identified 50 to 60 kinemes that he considered culturally universal, meaning that cultural differences in kinesic behavior come from variations within kinemes, not from the use of different kinemes.",
            "Kinemes are equivalent to morphemes and can be broken down further into kines, which are equivalent to phonemes, while variations in the intensity, duration, or extent of these behaviors are called allokines or kine variants, as when trained observers note as many as 23 different eyelid positions but only four positions appear to change the meaning of gaze behavior.",
            "Birdwhistell divided the body into eight regions, each with its own set of kinemes: total head, face, neck, trunk, shoulders/arms/wrists, hand and finger activity, hips/legs/ankles, and foot activity (including walking).",
            "According to Birdwhistell (1970), kinemes-in-use are combined into kinemorphs, which are similar to words in the linguistic system, and kinemorphs often occur in concert with other kinemorphs to produce complex kinemorphic constructions similar to sentences, the level at which the meaning of kinesic behavior becomes fully understandable.",
            "Parakinesics, similar to syntax in language, include kinesic markers, stress, and juncture, so that subtle head movements serve as markers, such as gesturing backward slightly with the head when talking in past tense and nodding forward when using future tense to describe future plans.",
            "Two muscle groups are involved in smiling, the zygomatic major (lip corners) and orbicularis oculi (eye corners), and experiments, perceptual observations, EMGs, and automated facial image analyses show that the Duchenne (spontaneous/genuine) smile differs from deliberately posed ones in terms of the onset, offset, duration, and possibly asymmetry of the muscle groups that are engaged.",
            "Posed smiles, also called semblances or social smiles, when compared to spontaneous smiles (symptoms), are 'turned on and off' more quickly but are longer in duration, are 'bigger,' tend to be more lopsided, and are less likely to engage the corners of the eyes.",
            "Ekman and Friesen (1969) proposed five distinct types of gestures, emblems, illustrators, regulators, affect displays, and adaptors, each of which performs different functions or displays different meanings, and these categories are not mutually exclusive, as a single gesture may serve more than one function at the same time.",
            "Illustrators (also called gesticulations) are kinesic acts accompanying speech that aid in the description of what is being said, trace the direction of speech, set the rhythm of speech, and gain and hold a listener's attention, with the Ekman and Friesen (1969) taxonomy including pictographs, kinetographs, ideographs, spatial movements, emblematic movements, deictic movements, batons, and rhythmic movements.",
            "There are three types of adaptors, self-adaptors (such as scratching your arm, playing with your hair, working your jaw, biting your lip, or picking your nose), alter-directed adaptors (movements directed toward another person during interpersonal interactions), and object adaptors (such as tapping a pencil while concentrating or playing with your phone), distinguished by the target of the behavior.",
            "Regulators are kinesic behaviors designed to manage or regulate turn taking between two or more interactants, learned at a very early age, displayed at a low level of awareness, and produced almost automatically, with the normal amount of gaze in an interaction ranging from 28% to 70% of the time, increasing when listening and decreasing when speaking.",
            "A 'blob' is what computer vision experts call the ellipses their techniques superimpose on human body parts: computers scan the pixels in a video, use color look-up-tables that match skin colors, form a bounding box and then an ellipse around the head, and use the head's position along with knowledge of the body's geometry to infer where the hands, forearms, shoulders, trunk, and legs are likely to be located within the frame.",
        ],
    ),
    "boss": (
        "Boss Level: The Voice, Vocalics, and the Power of Sound",
        "Your mission is to defeat the final boss of the auditory code: defend why the authors prefer the label vocalics over paralanguage, prosody, and noncontent speech, then unlock the machinery, the ear's 16 to 20,000 Hz range, the voice print that rivals the fingerprint, and a voice ranked second only to kinesics in communicative power. March through Trager's voice set, the eight elocutionary features plus dialect and fluency, the vocal emblems and alternants, the silences that make a person a 'nonperson,' Poggi's mind markers and cognitive state indicators, the acoustics of anger, the attractive voice, BBC English, and the leakage that gives liars away.",
        "A boss arena shaped like a sound wave: a spectrogram towers as the boss while an ear receives 16 to 20,000 Hz, a voice print freezes mid-analysis next to a fingerprint, Trager's voice set forms the backdrop, 'uh-huh,' 'shh' and 'tsk-tsk' float as emblems, a silent table of wait staff disappears into the background, and a newscaster's BBC English broadcasts from a monitor.",
        [
            "The authors prefer the label vocalics because it encompasses any vocal-auditory behavior except the spoken word, while 'paralanguage' is in some usages unnecessarily broad (encompassing kinesics) and in other respects too narrow, 'prosody' is usually linked to the words that are spoken, and 'noncontent speech' inaccurately implies that vocal aspects have no content.",
            "The human ear is capable of perceiving frequencies ranging from 16 to 20,000 Hz, and humans can recognize incredibly minute variations in sound, to the point that law enforcement and speech scientists have worked to perfect a voice print that would rival the fingerprint in producing reliable identification of people.",
            "The voice is probably second only to kinesics in the ranking of nonverbal codes on their communicative power, it is thought to have preceded formal language as a means of communication and been retained throughout evolution, and it has primacy in human development, since an infant begins to communicate with vocal cues long before words appear.",
            "In Trager's (1958, 1961) structural classification system, analysis begins with voice set, which consists of the mental, physiological, and physical characteristics of a speaker, including sex, age, state of health, body build, mood, bodily condition, and geographic location, against which all vocalic and speech behaviors are judged.",
            "Proponents of the elocutionary movement identified vocal features that a good speaker was expected to master, including pitch and pitch variation, inflection (the tonal patterns of the voice), loudness, emphasis, time or tempo, pauses, voice coloring, and voice quality such as clear, harsh, mellow, tremulous, or nasal.",
            "To the eight elocutionary vocal features, 20th-century researchers added dialect (the presence of accented speech) and fluency (the presence or absence of dysfluencies), the latter subdivided into the filled-pause variety, the 'ums' and 'ahs' that substitute for where a silent pause would occur, and other dysfluencies like unfilled pauses, repetitions, stutters, and garbled or incoherent sounds such as clicks and snorts.",
            "Vocalizations that stand alone and have well-understood symbolic meaning within a culture are considered vocal emblems, such as Trager's 'uh-huh' and 'hun-uh' for yes and no, and paralinguistic alternants like the rapid trilling of the lips that expresses delight from a child but disappointment or disgust from an adult, 'shh' to hush someone, and 'tsk-tsk' to chide someone.",
            "Silences are a particularly powerful symbolic message: they can be used to express negation without having to say 'no,' establish a sense of being aligned or in harmony with another, or transform the recipient into a 'nonperson,' as when teenagers subject a disliked classmate to total silence or a person stops speaking to a former friend as a forceful message that they have been 'excommunicated.'",
            "Poggi (2002a) introduced the concept of 'mind markers,' indications through voice, gesture, and other nonverbal signals that reveal a speaker's thoughts, goals, plans, and emotions, such as a 'hrmpp' that reveals disgruntlement at a proffered solution or a cooed 'oooh' that reveals pleasure in response to exciting news.",
            "Acoustically, anger is displayed through higher intensity, higher mean fundamental frequency, greater F0 range and variability, downward contours, higher-frequency energy, and clearer articulation, showing that emotional states can be described in terms of acoustic-prosodic combinations.",
            "Zuckerman and Miyake (1993) found that voices perceived as the most attractive had higher articulation and resonance, low to moderate shrillness, moderate pitch, small to medium pitch range, and low nasality.",
            "In the twentieth century, accent training became an unofficial requisite of the British educational system, and many newscasters for the British Broadcasting Corp. adopted the accent of their Oxford masters, which connoted intelligence and distinction and acquired the nickname BBC English, while newscasters in the United States are trained to remove any sign of an accent and speak general American dialect.",
        ],
    ),
}
